
[center][b][size=4]This is an extremely complicated mod, there are likely still some issues I haven't found, but I have found no game breaking issues after more than 40 hours of testing. Also I'm stuck on steamdeck for the next few weeks so please upload your own pics and videos![/size]
[/b]
[/center]
[line]

MFO lets you decide what your followers do on their own. You give each follower a short list of rules and they act on them, in a fight and out of it. It's the gambit system from Final Fantasy XII, rebuilt on Skyrim's own AI.

A rule is just [b][Condition] -> [Action][/b]. An enemy drops below 30% health, so power attack. You're out of health potions, so drink one when you get the chance. It's dark, so cast Candlelight. You stack the rules top to bottom and the first one that fits is the one that runs. From rules you write, followers can cast, attack, heal, drink potions, restock, loot, walk over to loot, and trade at merchants.

[size=5][b]How to use it[/b][/size]

You get a power called [b]Field Orders[/b]. Use it to open the board, pick a follower, and build their list. Each follower keeps two separate lists, one for combat and one for logistics like looting and trading, and you write both in game with a controller or a mouse. The board shows a plain-language summary of every rule. What a follower can be told to do depends on their own skills and spells, so a mage opens up cast rules a plain warrior won't have.

The behaviours are toggles and sliders in the MCM ([b]Mod Configuration -> marth's Follower Overhaul[/b]), and most of them are on out of the box. A follower still does nothing until you write rules for it, so start by giving one a couple of gambits and go from there.

[size=5][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]SKSE64[/*]
[*]Address Library for SKSE Plugins[/*]
[*]SkyUI[/*]
[*]MCM Helper (version 9 or newer)[/*]
[*]powerofthree's Papyrus Extender, only for the optional follower economy (selling and buying). Everything else runs without it.[/*]
[*]No DLC required.[/*]
[/list]

This needs Anniversary Edition (1.6.x). Skyrim SE 1.5.x and VR are not supported.

[size=5][b]Install[/b][/size]

[b]1.[/b] Install it with your mod manager like any other mod and enable [b]MFO.esp[/b]. Load order doesn't matter.

[b]2.[/b] (Recommended) Run the included [b]Synthesis[/b] patcher once, so the gambit item categories (potions, ammo, gear) cover your modded items. Grab the [b]MFO.synth[/b] file from Optional Files and double-click it in Synthesis, or add a Git patcher for [url=https://github.com/marthofdoom/MFO]github.com/marthofdoom/MFO[/url]. Without it MFO still works off a shipped catalog.

[b]3.[/b] Launch through SKSE, open the MCM to set it up, and use the Field Orders power to start writing rules.

[size=5][b]FAQ[/b][/size]

[b]Will it conflict with my follower framework, AI overhaul, or combat mod?[/b]
No. MFO sits on top of whatever manages your followers and acts on anyone flagged as your teammate, through runtime AI. It edits no follower records and no AI packages, and a follower with no matching rule behaves exactly like one without MFO.

[b]Does it work with modded followers and modded items?[/b]
Yes. Followers are found by teammate status, not a list, and item categories (potions, ammo, gear) come from the Synthesis catalog of your load order, so modded gear is judged by the same rules with no per-mod patch.

[b]Do I have to run Synthesis?[/b]
It's recommended but not required. Without it MFO uses a shipped catalog. With it the catalog is rebuilt from the mods you actually run.

[b]Why won't my follower cast the spell I set?[/b]
Competence is not permission. If he doesn't have the magicka or the skill, the rule fails and the next one is tried.

[b]Can I turn things off or tune it?[/b]
Yes. Everything ships off or conservative. Looting, the economy, auto-retreat, the confidence leash, dibs timing, and the rest are MCM toggles and sliders.

[b]Is it safe to add mid-playthrough?[/b]
Yes. All the state lives in the SKSE co-save and reverts cleanly, so you can add or remove MFO whenever.

[b]How can I develop an add-on?[/b]
You don't need C++. An add-on is a normal ESP or ESL that declares its content in records MFO reads at game load, so there's no DLL to write and nothing to patch in MFO itself. The follower progression add-on is the worked example. The full API is written up in ADDON-API.md in the [url=https://github.com/marthofdoom/MFO]GitHub repo[/url].

[size=5][b]Roadmap[/b][/size]

[b]Town errands are next.[/b] Followers heading into town on their own business, walking to merchants and doors and buying the gear and supplies they want while they're there. It extends the walk-to-loot movement to full town navigation.

[b]Deeper interop.[/b] Compatibility contracts with follower levelling and equipment-visibility mods as their APIs allow.

